---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import CtaLink from '../components/CtaLink.astro';
import StatStrip from '../components/StatStrip.astro';
import MethodList from '../components/MethodList.astro';
import Portrait from '../components/Portrait.astro';
import SampleDashboard from '../components/SampleDashboard.astro';
import ClosingCta from '../components/ClosingCta.astro';
import { CTA_MICROCOPY_HOME } from '../content/site';

const problems = [
  { heading: 'Leads that never became customers.', body: "The dashboard climbs. The bank account doesn't." },
  { heading: "Calls the platform can't see.", body: 'The phone is where the money is decided.' },
  { heading: 'Budget going to the wrong people.', body: 'The platform is doing what it was told.' },
  { heading: 'Cost per lead looks like progress.', body: 'It can fall while revenue falls with it.' },
];

const method = [
  'Establish what a customer is worth. Every decision after that depends on it.',
  "Connect the sale back to its source. That means call tracking, CRM data, and a hard look at what's being counted.",
  'Rebuild the campaigns around revenue. Same budget, different people.',
  "Report on what actually happened, including what I can't attribute yet.",
];
---

<Base
  title="Project Pineapple | Marketing for businesses where the sale happens in person"
  description="Paid advertising and marketing leadership for service businesses. Flat fee, revenue-based measurement, run by an operator. Salt Lake City, clients nationwide."
>
  <Header variant="full" wordmarkAsLink={false} />

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="eyebrow hero__eyebrow">Marketing for businesses where the sale happens in person</div>
    <h1>Somewhere between your ad spend and your bank account, the story gets lost.</h1>
    <p class="hero__sub">
      If your customers call or walk in, that's where it happens. The ad platform never sees the sale, so your
      agency reports on leads, because that's what it can see. I connect the spend to the customers who actually
      paid, so you can see which ads are worth paying for.
    </p>
    <CtaLink variant="button-lg" />
    <div class="hero__micro">{CTA_MICROCOPY_HOME}</div>
  </section>

  <StatStrip
    items={[
      { label: 'Revenue fit', value: '$500K–$10M' },
      { label: 'Media', value: '$3K+ / month' },
      { label: 'Fee', value: 'Flat monthly' },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="section section--pine problem">
    <h2 class="h2--dark">Where the story gets lost.</h2>
    <p class="problem__intro">
      Google spends your money toward whatever it can count. If it can't count the sale, it counts something else
      and calls that the result.
    </p>
    <div class="problem__grid">
      {
        problems.map((p) => (
          <div>
            <span class="diamond" aria-hidden="true" />
            <div class="problem__heading">{p.heading}</div>
            <div class="problem__body">{p.body}</div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="how-i-work" class="section method">
    <h2 class="eyebrow">How I work</h2>
    <MethodList items={method} />
  </section>

  <section class="section section--cream2 proof">
    <h2 class="h2">What this looks like in practice</h2>
    <div class="proof__stories">
      <p class="body body--long">
        A dental practice had been paying an agency for leads for about a year. The account was optimizing toward
        form submissions, and form submissions were coming in. When I connected the phone and the booking data
        back to the campaigns, most of those forms had never become appointments. The platform had gotten very
        good at finding people who fill out forms. Rebuilding around booked patients changed who the same budget
        reached, and the practice started seeing the result where it counts, in the schedule.
      </p>
      <p class="body body--long">
        A law firm was bringing in cases from several sources at once: an agency sending leads, directory
        listings, referrals, and their own paid search. Nobody could say which source the good cases came from.
        Once every source was tracked through to intake, one of them clearly wasn't producing, and the firm ended
        that contract. Their sales cycle is eight months. Three months in, I could tell them exactly what had
        entered the pipeline and where it came from. I could not tell them what had closed, because nothing had
        closed yet. I said so. Most agencies would have reported the pipeline number and let it sit there looking
        like revenue.
      </p>
    </div>
    <SampleDashboard />
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <div class="split__grid">
      <div class="split__col">
        <h2 class="eyebrow col-eyebrow">How I report</h2>
        <p class="body">
          I'll tell you what I know and what I don't. A report that overstates what it knows makes the number look
          good and makes the next decision worse. If your sales cycle is long, I'll be reporting on pipeline before
          I can report on revenue, and I'll say which is which.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split__col">
        <h2 class="eyebrow col-eyebrow">How I charge</h2>
        <p class="body">
          A flat monthly fee. Most agencies charge a percentage of your ad spend, which means they earn more when
          you spend more. That is a bad incentive to have sitting between you and the person advising you on your
          budget. My fee is the same whether I tell you to double the spend or cut it in half, so the
          recommendation is just the recommendation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="operator">
    <div class="operator__portrait">
      <Portrait crop="circle" size={150} />
    </div>
    <div class="operator__text">
      <div class="eyebrow">Adam Malmborg</div>
      <p class="operator__lead">Running paid advertising since 2014. Running businesses just as long.</p>
      <p class="body operator__body">
        I've made payroll, set pricing, hired and fired, and watched a marketing invoice land on a P&amp;L I was
        responsible for. That changes how I read an account. Not clicks and impressions, but what it cost to get a
        customer and whether that customer was worth getting.
      </p>
      <p class="operator__quote">You work with me, not an account team.</p>
      <a href="/about" class="textlink operator__link">More about me</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section engagement">
    <h2 class="h2">What working together looks like</h2>
    <div class="engagement__grid">
      <p class="body">
        Most clients want their paid advertising run properly, with measurement they can actually trust. That's
        the majority of what I do, and for a lot of businesses it's the right amount.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        Some want more than that. Someone to own the marketing function, sit in the decisions about pricing and
        capacity and hiring, evaluate every lead source rather than just the paid ones, and hold their other
        vendors accountable. That's a fractional CMO engagement, and it's a different scope.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="pull engagement__pull">
      You don't have to figure out which one you need before we talk. The assessment usually makes it obvious.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="who-its-for" class="section section--pine fit">
    <h2 class="h2">Who this is for</h2>
    <div class="fit__text">
      <p class="fit__p1">
        Businesses doing between $500,000 and $10 million in revenue, spending at least $3,000 a month on media,
        where the sale happens on a phone call, in a showroom, or across a few weeks of consideration. Dental and
        medical practices, law firms, med spas, custom retail.
      </p>
      <p class="fit__p2">
        It's a poor fit for pure ecommerce, where the platform already sees the transaction, and for high-volume
        low-ticket businesses where there isn't much to attribute.
      </p>
      <p class="fit__p3">Based in Salt Lake City, working with clients anywhere in the country.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <ClosingCta heading="Start with the assessment">
    Thirty minutes on what's being counted, what's wrong, and the two or three changes that would move the most.
    You keep the findings either way.
  </ClosingCta>

  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  /* Hero: the only centered block apart from closing CTAs */
  .hero {
    padding: var(--pad-hero) var(--gutter) clamp(44px, 7vw, 84px);
    text-align: center;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }
  .hero__eyebrow {
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin-bottom: 30px;
  }
  .hero h1 {
    margin: 0 auto 28px;
    font: 400 clamp(31px, 4.41vw, 52px) / 1.14 var(--serif);
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
    max-width: 24ch;
  }
  .hero__sub {
    margin: 0 auto 38px;
    max-width: 52ch;
    font: 400 18px/1.68 var(--sans);
    color: var(--text-72);
  }
  .hero__micro {
    margin: 16px auto 0;
    font: 400 13px/1.6 var(--sans);
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: 44ch;
  }

  /* Problem, on pine */
  .problem h2 {
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }
  .problem__intro {
    margin-bottom: 44px;
    max-width: 52ch;
    font: 400 16px/1.7 var(--sans);
    color: var(--on-pine-2);
  }
  .problem__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 290px), 1fr));
    gap: clamp(17px, 2.9vw, 34px) 60px;
  }
  .problem__grid .diamond {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }
  .problem__heading {
    font: 500 17px/1.4 var(--sans);
    margin-bottom: 7px;
  }
  .problem__body {
    font: 400 14.5px/1.65 var(--sans);
    color: var(--on-pine-4);
  }

  /* Method */
  .method {
    padding-bottom: var(--pad-secondary);
  }
  .method .eyebrow {
    margin-bottom: 30px;
  }

  /* Proof */
  .proof h2 {
    margin-bottom: 36px;
  }
  .proof__stories {
    display: grid;
    gap: 30px;
    max-width: 64ch;
  }

  /* How I report / How I charge */
  .split {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }
  .col-eyebrow {
    margin-bottom: 22px;
  }

  /* Operator block, home variant */
  .operator {
    padding: var(--pad-secondary) var(--gutter);
    background: var(--sand);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: clamp(96px, 13vw, 150px) 1fr;
    gap: var(--gutter-inner);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .operator__text {
    max-width: 58ch;
  }
  .operator__text .eyebrow {
    margin-bottom: 20px;
  }
  .operator__lead {
    margin: 0 0 20px;
    font: 400 clamp(14px, 2.03vw, 24px) / 1.42 var(--serif);
  }
  .operator__body {
    margin: 0 0 18px;
    color: rgba(34, 64, 47, 0.8);
  }
  .operator__quote {
    margin: 0 0 20px;
    padding-left: 18px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--clay);
    font: 400 16px/1.7 var(--sans);
  }
  .operator__link {
    font: 500 14.5px/1 var(--sans);
  }

  /* Engagement model */
  .engagement h2 {
    margin-bottom: 32px;
  }
  .engagement__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 290px), 1fr));
    gap: var(--gutter-inner);
    max-width: 1000px;
  }
  .engagement__pull {
    margin-top: 32px;
    max-width: 52ch;
  }

  /* Who this is for, on pine */
  .fit h2 {
    margin-bottom: 32px;
    max-width: 24ch;
  }
  .fit__text {
    display: grid;
    gap: 24px;
    max-width: 62ch;
  }
  .fit__p1 {
    font: 400 16px/1.75 var(--sans);
    color: var(--on-pine);
  }
  .fit__p2 {
    font: 400 16px/1.75 var(--sans);
    color: var(--on-pine-4);
  }
  .fit__p3 {
    font: 400 14.5px/1.7 var(--sans);
    color: var(--on-pine-5);
  }
</style>
